{
  "id": "f5267b6e-bc0b-4405-be28-7660fca7ef2e",
  "page_title": "How to work with prompt engineering and the Completion API",
  "page_description": "Learn how to work with the Completion API for prompt engineering.",
  "page_date": "02/16/2024",
  "chunk_title": "Add clear syntax",
  "chunk_content": "Add clear syntax  Using clear syntax for your prompt—including punctuation, headings, and section markers—helps communicate intent and often makes outputs easier to parse.  In the example below, separators (`---` in this case) have been added between different sources of information or steps. This allows the use of `---` as a stopping condition for generation. In addition, section headings or special variables are presented in uppercase to differentiate them.  |Prompt|Completion| |------|----------| |`You will read a paragraph, and then issue queries to a search engine in order to fact-check it. Also explain the queries.`<br>`---`<br>`PARAGRAPH`<br>`John Smith is married to Lucy Smith. They have five kids, and he works as a software engineer at Microsoft. What search queries should I do to fact-check this?`<br>`---`<br>`QUERIES` |`1. \"John Smith Microsoft\" - To check if John Smith is indeed employed at Microsoft.`<br>`2. \"John Smith Lucy Smith\" - To check if John Smith is married to Lucy Smith.`<br>`3. \"John Smith children\" - To check if John Smith has five children.`|  If you’re not sure what syntax to use, consider using markdown or XML,  since large language models have been trained on a lot of web content in XML or markdown.",
